$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM General/Typical Procedure: N4-Benzylspermidine (2b); Typical Procedure:
Sodium hydroxide (14 g, 0.35 mol) is dissolved in 95% ethanol (250 ml)
with vigorous stirring. Freshly distilled
N-(2-cyanoethyl)-N-(3-cyanopropyl)-benzylamine (1 b; 71 g, 0.313 mol) is
added and the two phase solution placed in a 500 ml glass hydrogenation
vessel. This mixture is treated with Raney nickel (10 g) weighed out as a
dense slurry in water (pH 10) and placed under hydrogen (40 psi ∼ 2.7
atm) in a Parr hydrogenation apparatus. After shaking until hydrogen
uptake ceases (which requires refilling the hydrogen reservoir), the
mixture is carefully vacuum filtered through sintered glass and the
catalyst washed with 95 % ethanol (2 × 25 ml). The used catalyst is
transferred, using an alkaline aqueous solution (pH 10), to a pooled
catalyst bottle where it can be stored under water (pH 10) and used
again. The filtrate from above is then concentrated to low volume (150
ml) and dissolved in water (300 ml). Saturated sodium hydroxide is added
until the amine begins to oil out and the mixture is then extracted with
chloroform (4 × 200 ml) until no more amine appears in the chloroform
extract (by T.L.C.). The combined organic layers are dried with sodium
sulfate and the solvent evaporated to give of N4-benzylspermidine; yield:
70.3 g (95 %). Although this material is pure enough for most uses, it
can be easily distilled. Reactant: 1d, Polyamine 2d, Yield: 81%. b.p:
found: 70-71 °C/0.25 torr, b.p: reported: 80-81 °C/1.0 torr.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):TITLE
$DATUM Amines and polyamines from nitriles
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):AUTHOR
$DATUM Bergeron, Raymond J.; Garlich, Joseph R.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):CITATION
$DATUM Synthesis (1984), (9), 782-4

